---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import MulchCalculator from '../../components/MulchCalculator.astro';
import { webApplicationSchema, breadcrumbSchema } from '../../utils/schema';
import { CALCULATORS_HUB } from '../../data/tools';

const path = '/calculators/mulch-calculator';
const title = 'Mulch Calculator: Cubic Yards & Bags';
const description =
  'Estimate mulch in cubic yards and bags for rectangular, circular, or known-area beds. Enter your planned depth and compare common bag sizes.';

const crumbs = [
  { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
  { label: CALCULATORS_HUB.title, href: '/calculators' },
  { label: 'Mulch Calculator' },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  schema={[
    webApplicationSchema({
      name: 'Mulch Calculator',
      description,
      path,
    }),
    breadcrumbSchema(crumbs),
  ]}
>
  <div class="container-site container-calc calc-page">
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />

    <header class="calc-page__intro">
      <p class="category-eyebrow calc-page__eyebrow"><a href="/calculators" class="text-brand no-underline hover:underline">Calculators</a></p>
      <h1 class="section-heading">Mulch Calculator</h1>
      <p class="section-lede calc-page__lede">
        Estimate how much mulch you need in cubic yards and bags for rectangular, circular, or known-area beds.
        Enter the depth you plan to use — this tool does not choose depth for you.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="calc-page__tool">
      <MulchCalculator />
    </div>

    <p class="calc-page__disclaimer">
      This calculator estimates mulch volume and bag counts from the area and depth you enter. It does not
      determine landscape design, drainage, plant suitability, soil health, fire-code compliance, or the required
      mulch depth for every plant or site. Confirm bag volumes on the product you buy and follow local horticultural
      guidance when needed. Keep mulch pulled back from tree trunks and stems.
    </p>

    <article class="prose-article calc-page__copy">
      <h2>How the mulch calculation works</h2>
      <p>
        The calculator converts your dimensions to feet, then computes volume in cubic feet. Rectangular beds use
        length × width × depth. Circles use π × radius² × depth. Known-area mode multiplies your area by depth.
        Cubic yards are cubic feet ÷ 27. Bag counts divide purchase volume by each bag’s cubic-foot size and round
        up. Optional overage is an editable percentage on top of the calculated volume — it defaults to 0%.
      </p>

      <h2>How many cubic yards of mulch do you need?</h2>
      <p>
        Multiply the bed area by the planned depth to get volume, then convert to cubic yards. For example, a
        10 × 10 ft bed at 2 inches deep is about 0.62 yd³ (16.67 ft³). Use the calculator for unit conversion and
        bag rounding in one step.
      </p>

      <h2>Mulch bags vs bulk</h2>
      <p>
        Retail mulch is often sold in bags labeled by cubic feet. Common package volumes include about 1.5, 2.0, and
        3.0 cu ft — these are examples, not universal products. Confirm the cubic-foot volume printed on the bag you
        buy. Bulk delivery is often more practical for larger projects; suppliers set their own minimums and delivery
        fees. This calculator does not price materials or recommend a bulk cutoff.
      </p>

      <h2>How deep should mulch be?</h2>
      <p>
        University extension and arboriculture guidance often discusses roughly 2–4 inches for landscape beds and
        trees, depending on material and site conditions. Finer mulches are often kept toward the lower end of that
        range; coarser mulches toward the higher end. Exact depth varies by plants, drainage, and purpose — enter the
        depth you plan to use rather than treating any single number as a requirement. Keep mulch pulled back from
        tree trunks and plant stems; do not create “mulch volcanoes” piled against bark.
      </p>

      <h2>How much area does one cubic yard cover?</h2>
      <p>
        Coverage depends only on depth: one cubic yard covers 27 ÷ depth_in_feet square feet. At common planning
        depths:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>2 in → about 162 sq ft</li>
        <li>3 in → about 108 sq ft</li>
        <li>4 in → about 81 sq ft</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The calculator derives coverage from the depth you enter rather than hardcoding a fixed table.
      </p>

      <h2>Example calculations</h2>
      <h3>10 × 10 ft bed, 2 in deep, no overage</h3>
      <p>
        Volume ≈ 0.62 yd³ (16.67 ft³). About 9 bags of 2 cu ft, 12 bags of 1.5 cu ft, or 6 bags of 3 cu ft. At this
        depth, one cubic yard covers about 162 sq ft.
      </p>
      <h3>20 × 30 ft bed, 3 in deep, no overage</h3>
      <p>
        Volume ≈ 5.56 yd³ (150 ft³). About 75 bags of 2 cu ft, 100 bags of 1.5 cu ft, or 50 bags of 3 cu ft. Coverage
        ≈ 108 sq ft per cubic yard.
      </p>
      <h3>Circular bed, 10 ft diameter, 3 in deep</h3>
      <p>
        Volume ≈ 0.73 yd³ (about 19.63 ft³). About 10 bags of 2 cu ft. Coverage remains ≈ 108 sq ft per cubic yard at
        3 inches.
      </p>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>How much mulch do I need?</h3>
      <p>
        Multiply area by planned depth to get volume, convert to cubic yards (÷ 27 from cubic feet), then divide by
        bag volume and round up. This calculator performs those steps from your inputs.
      </p>
      <h3>How many 2 cu ft bags are in a cubic yard?</h3>
      <p>
        One cubic yard is 27 ft³. Divided by 2 cu ft per bag equals 13.5 volume-equivalents, so you need 14 whole bags
        to meet or exceed one cubic yard.
      </p>
      <h3>How many square feet does one cubic yard cover?</h3>
      <p>
        It depends on depth. Use 27 ÷ depth_in_feet. At 2 inches, about 162 sq ft; at 3 inches, about 108 sq ft; at
        4 inches, about 81 sq ft.
      </p>
      <h3>How deep should mulch be?</h3>
      <p>
        Many extension sources discuss roughly 2–4 inches for landscape beds and trees, but appropriate depth varies
        by material, plants, and site. This calculator does not prescribe a single depth.
      </p>
      <h3>Can I use this calculator around trees?</h3>
      <p>
        Yes for quantity. Keep mulch pulled back from the trunk and root flare. The tool estimates volume only — it
        does not design tree rings or diagnose plant health.
      </p>
      <h3>Does mulch settle?</h3>
      <p>
        Organic mulch settles and decomposes over time. There is no universal settling factor built into this
        calculator. Check remaining depth before adding more so you do not keep stacking beyond a sensible range.
      </p>
      <h3>Should I order extra?</h3>
      <p>
        Optional overage defaults to 0%. You can add 5%, 10%, or a custom allowance for uneven beds or measurement
        variation. Bag counts already round up. Extra is not a settling or compaction model.
      </p>
      <h3>Is bulk mulch better than bags?</h3>
      <p>
        Bulk can be more practical for larger projects, depending on local price, minimums, and delivery fees. Compare
        your cubic-yard estimate with bag counts; this tool does not set a fixed bulk threshold or price materials.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.pubs.ext.vt.edu/content/dam/pubs_ext_vt_edu/426/426-724/426-724_pdf.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Virginia Cooperative Extension — Mulching for a Healthy Landscape (426-724)
          </a>
          — typical depth guidance for beds and woody plants; keep mulch off trunks
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://hgic.clemson.edu/factsheet/mulch/" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Clemson Cooperative Extension — Mulch
          </a>
          — maintain mulch depth guidance; space from stems and trunks
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.treesaregood.org/treeowner/treeownerinformation/propermulching" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            ISA / TreesAreGood — Proper Mulching Techniques
          </a>
          — recommended depth ranges; avoid mulch volcanoes
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://extension.illinois.edu/blogs/good-growing/2021-05-14-mulch-volcanoes-are-erupting-everywhere-heres-how-stop-them" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            University of Illinois Extension — Mulch volcanoes
          </a>
          — keep mulch away from trunks; depth context
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://extension.umn.edu/planting-and-growing-guides/mulching-trees-and-shrubs" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            University of Minnesota Extension — Mulching trees and shrubs
          </a>
          — depth ranges and mulch-free ring at the trunk
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://extension.psu.edu/mulching-landscape-trees" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Penn State Extension — Mulching Landscape Trees
          </a>
          — depth guidance and keeping mulch off the trunk/root flare
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://extension.umd.edu/resource/mulching-trees-and-shrubs" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            University of Maryland Extension — Mulching Trees and Shrubs
          </a>
          — material-dependent depth notes; keep mulch off trunks
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Related mulch guides</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/projects/how-much-mulch-do-i-need">How Much Mulch Do I Need?</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/guides/how-deep-should-mulch-be">How Deep Should Mulch Be?</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/materials/how-many-bags-of-mulch-in-a-cubic-yard"
            >How Many Bags of Mulch Are in a Cubic Yard?</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/projects/how-much-mulch-for-10x10-area"
            >How Much Mulch Do You Need for a 10×10 Area?</a
          >
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/materials/how-much-area-does-a-yard-of-mulch-cover"
            >How Much Area Does a Cubic Yard of Mulch Cover?</a
          >
        </li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .calc-page__copy :where(h2, h3) {
    color: var(--color-ink);
  }
</style>
